```
Reason: The statement uses `helper` which could be a user-supplied helper via `CTA_EXPECT_HELP_NAME` rather than the master conntrack’s helper. This allows the validation of `CTA_EXPECT_CLASS` against an incorrect `expect_class_max`, leading to an out-of-bounds access when the actual master helper has a smaller class range.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
helper = rcu_dereference(help->helper);
if (!helper)
    return ERR_PTR(-EOPNOTSUPP);

if (cda[CTA_EXPECT_CLASS]) {
    class = ntohl(nla_get_be32(cda[CTA_EXPECT_CLASS]));
    if (class > helper->expect_class_max)
        return ERR_PTR(-EINVAL);
}
```
Reason: The fix retrieves the helper exclusively from the master conntrack’s `help` structure (via `rcu_dereference(help->helper)`), ensuring that only the actual helper’s `expect_class_max` is used for validation. It also removes the earlier code that allowed userspace to specify a different helper name via `CTA_EXPECT_HELP_NAME`, thus preventing the mismatch that caused the bug.

